summary:
I recommend accepting this change. It adds one design note, docs/design/agent-and-build-slots.md, and nothing else. I checked its figures against the evidence snapshot (only the snapshot; I did not touch the real repository) and found no errors:
- the status --snapshot line (1069.2 min landing and rebuild, 14 landings on 2026-11-04, 174 landing records);
- the set of 14 landed jobs and the 9 it leaves out;
- the four directories it excludes for never running a model, and the count of 10 audience and 6 steward directories;
- all invocation and check durations for jobs b4f5, hsg4 and 911m, the table totals and percentages, and the three wait gaps in b4f5;
- the 19 landing records that list b4f5, and the first landing record for each sampled job;
- the six "low disk" outcome files;
- the code it cites: `.max()` in status.rs landing_record_time, ESTIMATE_T_BYTES of 6 GB, ESTIMATE_S_BYTES of 0.2 GB, MAX_CONCURRENCY_CEILING of 8, DEFAULT_MIN_FREE_GB of 10, and max_check_slots appearing only in config.rs and disk.rs.

The note keeps agent slots (model work) apart from build slots, which are queued with stated ordering and timeouts. It compares five ways to share compiled output and records the buy-first call. The sizing arithmetic is shown, and every unmeasured number is labeled an estimate or stated. The seven-step build order names the files each step touches and the metric that shows its effect. There are two minor findings. First, there is no CHANGELOG entry, although earlier design notes got one; the request asked for a single new file, so this is defensible. Second, the plan to clean a build slot's workspace-crate output after every command would force a producer to rebuild the workspace crates on every cargo command, a cost the note does not weigh.
